 "findings": [
  "Converter area: NeuroSim gives 255 um2 for a 12-bit SAR at 22 nm, 223 at 14 nm, 68 at 7 nm. Our assumed converter area (400 um2 at the design point) is therefore conservative, and 200 um2 is plausible at 14 nm and below.",
  "10-bit converters (enough per the bits test) are about 15% smaller and about 25% lower energy than 12-bit in NeuroSim's model.",
  "Energy: NeuroSim's converter plus column read is about 1 to 1.5 pJ per conversion; our periphery energy is 48.4 pJ per output, anchored on a measured chip. Against NeuroSim's circuit view our energy figures are pessimistic; real chips measure above idealised circuit fits, so we keep the measured anchor.",
  "Scope: this checks one component (the converter), not the whole chip. NeuroSim's whole-chip mode assumes bit-serial inputs and 1-bit weight slices, a different architecture from our pulse-width design (see E17b)."
 ],
